1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:07,860
Most nonprofits are trapped in a zero-sum game. They're like starving dogs fighting over a single bowl of food while an

2
00:00:07,860 --> 00:00:13,280
entire banquet sits untouched behind them. The dogs are very focused on the bowl.

3
00:00:13,820 --> 00:00:21,450
They've developed sophisticated bowl-fighting strategies. Some dogs have hired consultants to optimize their bowl access.

4
00:00:21,900 --> 00:00:27,840
None of them have turned around. Every grant you win is a grant another organization loses.

5
00:00:28,580 --> 00:00:32,640
Every dollar you secure for malaria is a dollar not spent on Alzheimer's.

6
00:00:33,940 --> 00:00:37,800
Every donor you convince is a donor distracted from climate change.

7
00:00:38,440 --> 00:00:44,300
Your success requires someone else's failure. You're not solving scarcity.

8
00:00:44,360 --> 00:00:49,020
You're reshuffling it while writing very professional reports about the reshuffling.

9
00:00:49,840 --> 00:00:59,920
Meanwhile, the real driver of scarcity, the real reason the bowl is so small, is ignored: Humanity spends 604 times more

10
00:00:59,980 --> 00:01:05,099
on weapons than on clinical trials to discover which medicines actually work.

11
00:01:05,980 --> 00:01:10,320
The bottleneck isn't basic research or lab equipment. It's testing.

12
00:01:11,180 --> 00:01:19,540
Your species has explored less than one percent of possible drug-disease combinations using existing safe compounds because

13
00:01:19,540 --> 00:01:26,920
clinical trials are too slow and expensive. This is the chokepoint that limits every health outcome.

14
00:01:27,340 --> 00:01:33,720
This is not an ideological claim. It's arithmetic. The kind where you multiply numbers together and get sad.

15
00:01:34,800 --> 00:01:39,620
Every year: Military spending is two point seven two trillion dollars.

16
00:01:40,340 --> 00:01:45,400
Global spending on curing disease is sixty-seven point five billion dollars.

17
00:01:46,100 --> 00:01:49,460
Military budgets are rising by hundreds of billions annually.

18
00:01:50,220 --> 00:01:59,200
Medical research budgets are facing REAL-TERM CUTS. The pie for solving disease, climate, poverty, and suffering is shrinking.

19
00:01:59,600 --> 00:02:08,240
The pie for building weapons is expanding. You cannot solve a resource allocation crisis by optimizing inside the smallest

20
00:02:08,240 --> 00:02:16,120
slice. That's like rearranging deck chairs on the Titanic, except the deck chairs are grants and the iceberg is two point

21
00:02:16,120 --> 00:02:22,860
seven two trillion dollars pointed at everyone's face. You must expand the pie.

22
00:02:23,460 --> 00:02:30,060
This turns a zero-sum game into a positive-sum game. This is the logic behind a one percent treaty.

23
00:02:30,820 --> 00:02:37,080
The Single Highest-R.O.I. Intervention Available. If your mission is: Curing Alzheimer's.

24
00:02:37,320 --> 00:02:41,620
Preventing cancer. Mitigating climate change. Slowing pandemics.

25
00:02:42,120 --> 00:02:47,400
Ending extreme poverty. Protecting democracy. Reducing existential risk.

26
00:02:47,920 --> 00:02:52,180
Improving education. Promoting peace. Stabilizing the biosphere.

27
00:02:52,740 --> 00:03:01,900
...the single highest-leverage thing you can do is: Help redirect one percent of global military budgets toward hyper-efficient

28
00:03:01,900 --> 00:03:10,620
pragmatic clinical trials. Why? Math. Every dollar spent on war is a dollar not spent on life.

29
00:03:11,180 --> 00:03:18,740
This is how money works. Every dollar moved from military to health has a 10 to 200 times higher welfare multiplier.

30
00:03:19,420 --> 00:03:24,880
Bombs have negative R.O.I. on human flourishing. Medicine has positive R.O.I.

31
00:03:25,240 --> 00:03:32,460
These are different numbers. Redirecting the flow of resources changes the future more than any single program.

32
00:03:33,040 --> 00:03:41,400
You can bail water forever or you can plug the hole. A healthier, wealthier, more stable world reduces every global problem

33
00:03:41,400 --> 00:03:47,290
simultaneously. It's like fixing the foundation instead of patching individual cracks.

34
00:03:47,860 --> 00:03:54,660
War is upstream of poverty. War is upstream of pollution. War is upstream of refugee crises.

35
00:03:55,620 --> 00:04:00,580
War is upstream of authoritarianism. War is upstream of disease spread.

36
00:04:01,520 --> 00:04:10,060
War is upstream of technological stagnation. War is upstream of climate destruction (militaries are among the largest polluters

37
00:04:10,060 --> 00:04:13,820
on Earth, which is ironic given they're supposedly protecting it).

38
00:04:14,680 --> 00:04:18,800
War is, in fact, upstream of basically everything you don't like.

39
00:04:19,100 --> 00:04:26,620
It's the root password to your worst problems. Reduce war by one percent, and every downstream mission gets easier.

40
00:04:26,740 --> 00:04:31,280
Increase war by one percent, and every mission becomes almost impossible.

41
00:04:32,040 --> 00:04:37,000
A one percent treaty is not a peace movement. It's a RESOURCE-REALLOCATION MOVEMENT.

42
00:04:37,820 --> 00:04:41,320
Nobody has to hold hands or sing songs. They just have to do math.

43
00:04:42,700 --> 00:04:49,320
Why This Applies to YOUR Organization. Environmental groups: Militaries are the largest polluters on Earth.

44
00:04:49,940 --> 00:04:56,920
Defunding them one percent is the most effective climate policy ever proposed, and it doesn't require anyone to give up

45
00:04:56,920 --> 00:05:03,960
hamburgers. Anti-poverty groups: War and disease are the root causes of most modern poverty and famine.

46
00:05:04,440 --> 00:05:11,940
A one percent treaty tackles both. Your current strategy of "ask rich people to feel bad" has been running for sixty years.

47
00:05:12,480 --> 00:05:18,380
How's that going? Peace organizations: Seventy years of moral appeals haven't worked.

48
00:05:18,940 --> 00:05:25,860
A one percent treaty makes peace more profitable than war. In a world run by greed, that's the only argument that matters.

49
00:05:27,160 --> 00:05:32,380
Disease advocacy groups: You're fighting other disease groups for crumbs of the N.I.H.

50
00:05:32,560 --> 00:05:38,980
budget. A one percent treaty bakes an entirely new pie with twelve point three times more trial capacity.

51
00:05:40,040 --> 00:05:45,640
The bottleneck isn't ideas or molecules; it's running enough trials to find out what works.

52
00:05:46,360 --> 00:05:51,760
This solves that. "But Shouldn't Nonprofits Focus on Their Core Mission?".

53
00:05:52,580 --> 00:05:56,320
Yes. That's exactly why they should support a one percent treaty.

54
00:05:56,900 --> 00:06:03,720
Every nonprofit today operates inside a system that guarantees scarcity: too few grant dollars.

55
00:06:04,100 --> 00:06:08,540
too much competition. too much fragmentation. political headwinds.

56
00:06:09,200 --> 00:06:13,750
donor fatigue. shrinking government budgets for health, climate, and poverty.

57
00:06:14,780 --> 00:06:22,260
ballooning budgets for war. Most nonprofits are fighting each other for scraps of a shrinking pie while the pie-shrinkers

58
00:06:22,260 --> 00:06:27,100
get two point seven two trillion dollars annually to shrink it faster.

59
00:06:27,960 --> 00:06:36,700
If your mission matters, this is suicidal strategy. A small increase in resources within your field leads to small, incremental

60
00:06:36,700 --> 00:06:42,500
improvements. More staff. More grants. More programs. More reports about the reports.

61
00:06:43,360 --> 00:06:50,760
A small increase in military budgets leads to arms races, instability, and the destruction of progress you spent decades

62
00:06:50,760 --> 00:06:59,060
building. If your organization does not help shift the resource flow, your future funding and impact will be determined

63
00:06:59,060 --> 00:07:06,670
by someone else. Specifically, by people whose job is "convince politicians that killing is profitable.".

64
00:07:07,280 --> 00:07:12,900
Supporting a one percent treaty is not a distraction. IT IS STRATEGIC SELF-PRESERVATION.

65
00:07:13,860 --> 00:07:22,060
The Zero-Sum Trap vs. The Positive-Sum Solution. Right now, every grant you win is a grant another org loses.

66
00:07:22,840 --> 00:07:30,280
The bucket is shrinking while you fight over it. A one percent treaty grows the bucket by twenty-seven point two billion

67
00:07:30,280 --> 00:07:37,160
dollars per year. Everyone gets funded. Malaria gets cured AND Alzheimer's gets cured.

68
00:07:37,920 --> 00:07:45,480
You stop writing proposals and start governing the fund. This is like discovering that instead of fighting over one pizza,

69
00:07:45,820 --> 00:07:54,120
you can just order infinite pizzas. Except the pizzas are billions of dollars and the ordering is international treaty ratification.

70
00:07:55,620 --> 00:08:03,540
"Why Now?" Because the Window Is Open. For the first time in 80 years, everything is going wrong at the same time.

71
00:08:04,360 --> 00:08:10,820
This is, paradoxically, your best opportunity: The public is angry about rising military budgets.

72
00:08:11,380 --> 00:08:16,380
Trust in government spending priorities is at "used car salesman" levels.

73
00:08:16,480 --> 00:08:24,460
Medical breakthroughs feel within reach but unrealized. Climate disasters have shifted public opinion from "debatable" to

74
00:08:24,460 --> 00:08:31,050
"my house is underwater." Pandemics exposed the cost of underfunding resilience.

75
00:08:31,560 --> 00:08:39,960
A.I. and biotech make accelerated progress possible. Leaders are searching for high-leverage, unifying narratives (and finding

76
00:08:39,960 --> 00:08:48,980
none, which is where you come in). You cannot pass a resource-reallocation treaty when people feel safe, rich, and complacent.

77
00:08:49,600 --> 00:08:54,180
Comfortable humans don't change things. Uncomfortable humans change everything.

78
00:08:55,220 --> 00:09:02,280
You can pass it when budgets are being cut, militaries are expanding, and people feel the system is misaligned.

79
00:09:02,980 --> 00:09:08,260
This is the moment. If nonprofits do not push now, the window closes.

80
00:09:08,660 --> 00:09:12,100
And when it closes, every mission becomes harder for decades.

81
00:09:12,740 --> 00:09:17,580
The dogs will still be fighting over the shrinking bowl while the banquet rots behind them.

82
00:09:19,160 --> 00:09:24,600
Historical Precedent: This Has Worked Before. During World War Two: The U.S.

83
00:09:24,600 --> 00:09:28,500
converted car factories into bomber factories. G.M. built tanks.

84
00:09:29,420 --> 00:09:35,140
Ford built B-twenty-fours. Frigidaire (yes, the refrigerator people) produced machine guns.

85
00:09:35,960 --> 00:09:40,620
Typewriter manufacturers made rifles. Not because they "supported war."

86
00:09:40,940 --> 00:09:44,720
Because the system needed realignment and ignoring it was impossible.

87
00:09:45,580 --> 00:09:51,720
Also because the government asked very firmly, in a tone that strongly implied "this isn't really a question.".

88
00:09:52,640 --> 00:09:58,040
Today, the world needs the opposite realignment: Less production of weapons.

89
00:09:58,180 --> 00:10:02,300
More production of cures, resilience, stability, and health.

90
00:10:03,060 --> 00:10:10,140
The global nonprofit sector is the modern equivalent of those car factories: massive capacity, talented people, pointed

91
00:10:10,140 --> 00:10:17,160
at the wrong problem. You have millions of smart, passionate humans optimizing spreadsheets for the wrong budget.

92
00:10:17,720 --> 00:10:22,420
It's like watching a Formula 1 pit crew change tires on a car that's driving toward a cliff.

93
00:10:23,440 --> 00:10:30,480
A few years of effort to shift one percent of global budgets could produce more impact than THIRTY YEARS of incremental

94
00:10:30,480 --> 00:10:35,300
programs. This is not optimism. This is arithmetic applied to resource flows.

95
00:10:36,240 --> 00:10:40,100
Why Not Just Run Decentralized Trials Ourselves? You should.

96
00:10:40,600 --> 00:10:44,760
But decentralized trials alone don't solve the fundamental resource problem.

97
00:10:45,940 --> 00:10:53,400
Decentralized trials scale infinitely better when the resource stream feeding them is ten to forty times larger.

98
00:10:54,100 --> 00:11:01,020
If you build the most efficient trial system in the world but the budget for clinical progress is shrinking, you still lose.

99
00:11:01,680 --> 00:11:05,200
You've built a beautiful garden hose attached to a pipe with no water.

100
00:11:05,960 --> 00:11:11,540
You cannot out-optimize a broken budget. You must change the resource flow itself.

101
00:11:12,300 --> 00:11:18,980
Build the infrastructure AND expand the funding. Do both. They're complements, not substitutes.

102
00:11:19,020 --> 00:11:27,040
A 1% Treaty Is a Rising Tide That Lifts Every Mission. What happens if the world shifts just one percent of military budgets?

103
00:11:27,940 --> 00:11:31,300
Health. Twelve point three times more clinical trial capacity.

104
00:11:32,300 --> 00:11:37,140
One hundred times faster therapeutic discovery. Lower costs per cure.

105
00:11:37,160 --> 00:11:41,780
Diseases get cured instead of managed (the pharmaceutical industry's least favorite sentence).

106
00:11:43,080 --> 00:11:49,480
Climate. Lower military emissions (militaries are massive polluters, but nobody carbon-taxes a tank).

107
00:11:50,340 --> 00:11:53,920
Fewer resource-driven conflicts. More funds for climate solutions.

108
00:11:54,800 --> 00:12:01,160
Poverty. Trillions in long-term economic productivity. Fewer war-displaced refugees.

109
00:12:01,780 --> 00:12:05,640
More government revenue for social services. Global Stability.

110
00:12:06,200 --> 00:12:11,240
Less chance of great-power conflict. More international cooperation on existential risks.

111
00:12:11,240 --> 00:12:15,780
Everyone slightly less dead (the lowest bar imaginable, and yet).

112
00:12:16,900 --> 00:12:22,900
Nonprofit Impact. Everyone stops competing for crumbs. Every mission receives more oxygen.

113
00:12:23,480 --> 00:12:29,940
Funders far more willing to invest. You stop writing grant proposals and start actually doing your mission.

114
00:12:30,500 --> 00:12:37,220
Remember your mission? It was in the brochure. A one percent move unlocks one hundred percent more possibility.

115
00:12:38,020 --> 00:12:44,650
The Ask Is Small, The Upside Is Massive. You're not asking for global disarmament or naive pacifism.

116
00:12:45,200 --> 00:12:50,560
You're asking for one percent. One penny of each tax dollar currently used to build weapons.

117
00:12:51,260 --> 00:12:55,800
One small shift in a two point seven two trillion dollar flow.

118
00:12:56,440 --> 00:13:00,160
Countries still keep ninety-nine percent of their apocalypse capacity.

119
00:13:00,520 --> 00:13:03,960
They can still end all life nineteen times instead of twenty.

120
00:13:04,840 --> 00:13:10,560
If you can't successfully end the world with nineteen attempts, the twentieth probably wasn't going to help.

121
00:13:11,320 --> 00:13:18,650
But that single percentage point: creates a twenty-seven point two billion dollar a year one percent Treaty Fund.

122
00:13:18,940 --> 00:13:25,740
funds global decentralized clinical trials. makes every nonprofit's mission ten times more feasible.

123
00:13:26,420 --> 00:13:31,880
It is the highest-leverage bargain available to humanity. On Bribing Nonprofits (Legally).

124
00:13:31,880 --> 00:13:38,060
Asking nonprofits to support a one percent treaty out of moral obligation is like asking a drowning person to rescue other

125
00:13:38,060 --> 00:13:45,440
swimmers. They need incentives, not guilt. You offer two. Make The Organization Rich.

126
00:13:45,440 --> 00:13:48,720
Old way: "Here's one hundred thousand dollars. Hope it helps.

127
00:13:49,280 --> 00:13:52,980
Submit a forty-seven-page report in six months about impacts and learnings.

128
00:13:53,440 --> 00:14:01,060
Use the word 'stakeholder' at least fourteen times.". New way: "Here's fifty thousand dollars baseline to get started.

129
00:14:01,440 --> 00:14:07,680
Plus ten thousand dollars for every ten thousand verified referendum votes you drive through your unique link.

130
00:14:08,340 --> 00:14:16,280
No cap.". The nonprofit's most rational path to maximizing their budget becomes: be your most effective mobilization partner.

131
00:14:16,980 --> 00:14:25,300
One metric. No ambiguity. Get paid for results. This is how every functional industry works except charity, which explains

132
00:14:25,300 --> 00:14:28,380
why charity hasn't solved anything yet. Make The Leaders Personally Rich.

133
00:14:28,380 --> 00:14:31,560
Even performance grants get absorbed into general operations.

134
00:14:32,160 --> 00:14:39,320
"We hired three more grant writers!" Congratulations. You've invented a machine that converts donated money into more requests

135
00:14:39,320 --> 00:14:47,920
for donated money. The fix: Give leaders personal equity through VOTE points, future claims on the VICTORY Incentive Alignment

136
00:14:47,920 --> 00:14:55,360
Bonds and the one percent Treaty Fund. The Executive Director's personal wealth is now tied to treaty success.

137
00:14:55,700 --> 00:15:02,000
Their Tesla depends on it. Your species responds better to personal wealth than organizational mission statements.

138
00:15:03,000 --> 00:15:09,500
This is not a criticism. It's a design parameter. The Real Prize: Stop Begging Forever.

139
00:15:10,240 --> 00:15:13,100
The performance grants and VOTE points are just the appetizer.

140
00:15:13,820 --> 00:15:21,340
The real prize is never writing a grant proposal again. The one percent Treaty Fund receives twenty-seven point two billion

141
00:15:21,340 --> 00:15:30,020
dollars annually. Your decentralized institutes of health (D.I.H.) governs this fund, allocating multi-billion dollar budgets

142
00:15:30,020 --> 00:15:35,940
to specialized institutes: Mental Health, Aging, whatever issue your organization addresses.

143
00:15:36,380 --> 00:15:41,040
Who controls these billions? The coalition of nonprofits who made the treaty happen.

144
00:15:41,680 --> 00:15:47,900
You helped create it. You govern it. This is the nonprofit equivalent of "I don't want a fish.

145
00:15:48,480 --> 00:15:53,430
I want to own the lake.". Option A: Keep competing for finite grants forever.

146
00:15:53,880 --> 00:15:58,810
Write proposals. Beg foundations. Hope your competitor doesn't write a better proposal.

147
00:15:59,380 --> 00:16:07,440
Option B: Join the coalition. Help pass the treaty. Collectively govern a multi-billion dollar annual treasury dedicated

148
00:16:07,440 --> 00:16:13,860
to your mission. One involves begging. The other involves being the person everyone begs to.

149
00:16:14,760 --> 00:16:22,820
Earning Your Stake. A partner's governance stake is based on verified contributions during the campaign: funds raised, referendum

150
00:16:22,820 --> 00:16:28,600
votes driven, and in-kind contributions credited. Contribute more, govern more.

151
00:16:29,400 --> 00:16:35,680
It's the same incentive structure that makes capitalism work, except pointed at curing diseases instead of selling sugar

152
00:16:35,680 --> 00:16:39,610
water to people who already have diabetes. The Bottom Line.

153
00:16:40,120 --> 00:16:44,710
If you care about your mission, you must care about a one percent treaty.

154
00:16:45,120 --> 00:16:49,600
You cannot solve your mission inside a shrinking, war-distorted budget environment.

155
00:16:50,200 --> 00:16:53,580
You cannot optimize your way out of a fundamental resource mismatch.

156
00:16:54,820 --> 00:17:01,900
You cannot win a game where the rules guarantee your loss. You're playing chess on a board that's on fire, and someone keeps

157
00:17:01,900 --> 00:17:09,319
adding lighter fluid. The smartest thing the nonprofit sector can do right now: Stop fighting over scraps.

158
00:17:09,980 --> 00:17:15,400
Stop playing a zero-sum game. Grow the pie. Pass a one percent treaty.

159
00:17:15,960 --> 00:17:19,619
Then return to your mission with ten to forty times greater resources.

160
00:17:20,680 --> 00:17:29,500
This is not idealism. This is strategy. A few years of coordinated advocacy for a one percent treaty will unlock more impact

161
00:17:29,500 --> 00:17:36,560
across every field than decades of incrementalism. Decades during which, I should note, the problems got worse.

162
00:17:37,300 --> 00:17:41,180
So maybe incrementalism is just a polite word for "losing slowly.".

163
00:17:42,100 --> 00:17:47,180
The dogs need to turn around and notice the banquet. This is the moment to act.
